<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fatawa-i-Alamgiri</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Fatawa-i-Alamgiri is a compilation of Islamic law of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanafi-school">Hanafi school</a>, prepared by scholars under the patronage of the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> in the late seventeenth century. It reflected Aurangzeb's emphasis on Islamic orthodoxy and served as a legal reference in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fatawa-i-alamgiri.jpg" alt="Fatawa-i-Alamgiri" width="320" height="224" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: British Library Blog · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manuscript_copy_of_al-Fatawa_al-%27Alamgiriyyah.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
